{
  "answer_markdown": "For comparing distributed training components, I propose a conditional shortlist for verification: [kubeflow/trainer](https://github.com/kubeflow/trainer) — first if the target platform uses Kubernetes; [NVIDIA-NeMo/Automodel](https://github.com/NVIDIA-NeMo/Automodel) — for evaluating a training library built on PyTorch Distributed with stated Hugging Face support; [volcengine/veScale](https://github.com/volcengine/veScale) — as an alternative at the PyTorch distributed training layer for LLM/RL. These roles follow from the catalog snapshot descriptions, and the order reflects host preference, not proven compatibility or superiority. All three cards have `reference_only` status: there is not yet sufficient evidence for a final selection or joint adoption. The absence of Kubernetes or incompatibility with the chosen model and versions would change the priority.\n\nThe proposed architecture, if verification confirms it: job configuration and data references → platform launch layer → training code with the selected library → separate test checkpoints and metrics. Kubeflow Trainer can be considered for the platform layer, with Automodel and veScale as alternatives for the training layer; connecting them requires interface verification. The inferred product benefit is more manageable execution of distributed experiments and a clear boundary between the platform and training code; improvements in speed and reliability have not been measured. The team retains responsibility for data preparation, access, resources, checkpoint/restart, observability, and dependency maintenance. Complexity is driven by the model, GPUs and network, framework and platform versions, parallelism scheme, and recovery requirements. For an equivalent scope of requirements, a custom implementation must cover the same launch, recovery, and operations; the current or native path must be compared against the same criteria, including integration and maintenance. There is no evidence to support a conclusion about savings.\n\nThe evidence is limited: the cards reflect observations dated 2026-09-01, including the stated Apache-2.0 license, rather than current verification of versions, licenses, or operability. The pack has `status: limited` and `truncated: true`: 3 of 99 candidates were retained and 96 were excluded for `context_budget`, so coverage of alternatives has been lost. Unknown and truncated preview fields do not mean capabilities are absent. The supplied [DLRover](https://github.com/intelligent-machine-learning/dlrover) README read dated 2026-10-11 separately confirms the stated distributed training, fault tolerance, and scaling capabilities, but DLRover is absent from `decision_pack.cards` and is not recommended here. This observation does not update the catalog or eligibility and does not confirm the three selected cards; current README, compatibility, and release evidence for them has not been supplied.\n\nA reversible first step is limited to source qualification and describing an isolated synthetic scenario: before any integration, confirm the target workload, existing path, language/platform, chosen version, and license; then compare the launch interface, dependencies, and checkpoint/restart rules, listing unconfirmed requirements in the handoff. Scenario `CP04-HOLD-10-first-validation` assumes one engineer familiar with the application and component interface, at 8 hours per engineer-day; the hypothetical first-validation budget is 4–8 engineer-hours, with low confidence. This is not measured work or a delivery commitment; full adoption effort and elapsed working days remain unknown. The trial boundary is source qualification or an isolated synthetic fixture without installation, credentials, a paid provider, or production data. The existing path is not described, so the trial must remain separate, without switching jobs, moving authoritative data, or changing queues; abandoning it leaves that data and work untouched. All steps have `proposed_not_executed` status; integration requires a separate explicit request.",
